dracut-sshd (0.7.1-4~eugenesan~resolute3) resolute; urgency=medium

  * telegram-send: keep the notification off the boot's critical path
    - Add a runtime kill switch, rd.telegram-send=0 on the kernel command
      line: the script reads /proc/cmdline, logs "doing nothing" and exits 0
      before touching anything. A boot that hangs is exactly the case where
      there is no shell to stop the unit from, so the bisection has to be
      possible from the boot prompt. Deliberately a runtime check in the script
      rather than a dracut module exclusion: module-setup.sh runs at build
      time, where neither getargbool nor the target's command line exist
    - Bound a single run from the outside as well as from the inside: the unit
      had TimeoutStartSec=0, which removed systemd's backstop entirely, so a
      run wedged on something unexpected - a resolver that never answers, a
      read from a driver that never returns - would stay in sysinit.target for
      the whole boot with nothing able to kill it. It is now 180s, above the
      script's own bound, so a normal run is never killed mid-flight and a
      stuck one is
    - Make the wait honour its own bound. The deadline was checked before the
      sleep, so an unclamped backoff overshot it by a whole step - measured
      63s for a 60s budget and 15s for an 8s one. The nap is now capped at
      what is left of the budget, so a run that promises 60s takes 60s
    - Wait 60s for the network instead of 300s. A run that waits five minutes
      is a five-minute job sitting in sysinit.target while the initramfs is
      still bringing the machine up, and "the notification script is still
      running" is not something anyone wants to debug during a boot that is
      already in trouble. Nothing is lost by giving up early: the timer re-arms
      every 5min, a run that gives up records a failed attempt, and the message
      arrives within five minutes rather than one
    - Document the failure mode of the image itself in readme.dracut-modules:
      kernel_cmdline="rd.neednet=1", which this package ships for sshd, means a
      machine whose network configuration does not match the hardware it booted
      on never reaches the root filesystem, and the console shows the initqueue
      retrying a hook while dev-mapper-luks*.target sits with no limit. That is
      root unlock waiting, not the notification; the note also lists netplan
      name matching, the ip=dhcp fallback and the cost of dropping rd.neednet
  * telegram-send: rework the network check and the external address, after
    testing the change detection on real boots
    - Log which provider answered. The reachability check now names the
      endpoint that confirmed the network on every run instead of only after a
      failure, and the external address lookup names the service it used, so
      "ext is unknown today" can be told apart from "that service is down"
    - Actually rotate the reachability endpoints between runs. The counter
      started at zero and every run is a new process, so netchecks[0] answered
      every single time and the other five were dead weight on a healthy
      machine; it is now seeded randomly, and kept only so that a failing check
      moves on to the next endpoint within one run
    - Look the external address up once per run. Under
      TELEGRAM_EXT_CHECK=always gather_netinfo() runs twice, and the second
      call both repeated the request and cleared the value the first one had
      fetched, so the report path saw "unknown" and needlessly fell back to the
      cached address
    - Ask every external address endpoint for the address family of our own
      source address. On a dual-stack machine different operators answer with
      different families for the same host (api.ipify.org and
      checkip.amazonaws.com return IPv4, ifconfig.me, icanhazip.com and
      ident.me IPv6), so rotating between them flipped the field between v4
      and v6, the comparison read that as a change, and a dual-stack machine
      would have notified every few ticks about nothing
    - Report the last known external address in the log on a run that did not
      look it up, rather than printing "unknown" and making a working lookup
      look broken; likewise on a run that does report, a lookup that fails now
      keeps the cached value instead of replacing it with "unknown"
    - Query seven endpoints belonging to seven different operators
      (api.ipify.org, ifconfig.me/ip, checkip.amazonaws.com, icanhazip.com,
      ident.me, wtfismyip.com/text, ipinfo.io/ip), starting at a different one
      on every run, and accept a reply only if it really is a bare address, so
      an error page, a redirect or a captive portal can never reach the message
    - Notice the link being cycled, not just the addresses changing, and use
      it as the trigger for looking the external address up. Two free sysfs
      signals are recorded with the rest of the cache:
      carrier_down_count (carrier_changes on an older kernel), the kernel's
      tally of link losses, and statistics/rx_packets, which can only decrease
      if the interface was destroyed and recreated. The usual way an unattended
      machine ends up with a new external address is a router reboot, and that
      leaves interface, driver and source address untouched - routers hand the
      same address to the same MAC - so without these the new public address
      went unnoticed until something else changed too. Only a loss or a
      decrease counts, never growth, so a busy link does not turn into a
      request per tick. A detected cycle is reported in its own right, with or
      without an address change - the same router usually hands out the same
      address again, so requiring a difference would mean never reporting it -
      and the reason is added to the message as link:[...]; one cycle means one
      message, because the new counters are recorded with the attempt. Being
      read from the kernel alone, cycle detection is unaffected by
      TELEGRAM_SEND_IP=0, so cycling the link - from the far end of the cable,
      or by rebooting the router - forces a fresh status message whatever state
      the last attempt left behind
    - Rate limit those cycle-triggered messages to TELEGRAM_CYCLE_MAX (3) per
      TELEGRAM_CYCLE_WINDOW (3600s) seconds, counted over a rolling window so a
      burst across a boundary cannot double the allowance. Only a cycle that is
      the sole reason to report is ever suppressed: the initial status and any
      real change of interface, driver, address or message always get through,
      and TELEGRAM_CYCLE_MAX=0 keeps the detection but never notifies on it.
      This cap, not the timer's 5min interval, is what bounds how many messages
      a fault produces; the interval only decides how soon a change is noticed,
      and how long somebody pulling the cable has to wait for a status refresh
    - Treat the external address as advisory: a failed lookup reports "unknown"
      and never counts as a change, the last known value is kept instead of
      being overwritten, and a lookup happens only on a run that reports
      something anyway or the link cycled (TELEGRAM_EXT_CHECK=on-change, the
      default) - so a machine whose network is quiet asks no IP-echo service at
      all. "always" restores detection of an external-address-only change with
      no local sign at all, at one request per tick
    - Skip the send when the interface or source address is unknown, rather
      than reporting an empty identity and overwriting a good cache entry
    - Put the log and the state cache in one runtime directory chosen at
      runtime - $TELEGRAM_RUN_DIR, then $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (/run/user/<uid>),
      then /run when writable, then /tmp with the uid in the filename - so the
      script works for a regular user as well, and dropped the hardcoded /run
      paths and /root/.telegram_bot from the unit, which now works unchanged
      as a system unit and as a user unit
  * Ship debian/readme.dracut-modules.txt in the package documentation, so the
    netplan, network-manager-wireless, telegram-send and unlock-root modules
    are documented where an installed system can be read
  * debian/config/sshd.conf: add tail(1) to install_items, for following the
    telegram-send log from a rescue shell. No dracut module installs it -
    80base provides readlink and sleep, but not tail

 -- Eugene San (eugenesan) <eugenesan@gmail.com>  Tue, 06 Oct 2026 17:30:31 -0700

dracut-sshd (0.7.1-3~eugenesan~resolute3) resolute; urgency=medium

  * telegram-send: rewrite to fix a silent hang when booting without network
    - Replace the uninterruptible /dev/tcp probe (~135s per attempt) with
      bounded curl probes, add a bounded wait with logged retries, retry and
      verify delivery, and escape/encode the message so it is no longer
      dropped by HTTP 400
    - Drop nm-online, wait-online, wget, awk, readlink, basename, date and
      uname; depend on the url-lib module for curl and the CA bundle
    - Make the module skip cleanly when no token is configured instead of
      failing the dracut run
    - Run the notification as a one-shot unit: it is no longer resent on a
      timer, is not killed by the 90s start timeout, and no longer blocks root
      unlock while waiting for the network
    - Drive the repeat with a new telegram-send.timer (OnBootSec=5min,
      OnUnitActiveSec=5min) instead of Restart=: systemd does not allow
      Restart=always/on-success for oneshot units, so a repeating one-shot
      needs a timer. RemainAfterExit=yes had to go as well, because it keeps
      the unit "active (exited)" and OnUnitActiveSec would then never re-arm
    - Notify only on a real change: cache the last attempt and the network
      identity (interface, driver, source and external address, plus the
      message) in /run/telegram-send.state, so a tick that finds nothing new
      sends nothing. The cache lives on tmpfs, so the first run after every
      reboot still notifies, and a recorded failure always forces a retry. A
      failed wait is recorded with an empty identity, so a network that returns
      unchanged after an outage is still reported once
    - Probe a rotating list of public connectivity endpoints (Microsoft NCSI,
      Google gstatic, Apple, Ubuntu, Cloudflare, Amazon) before the Bot API,
      and only ask api.telegram.org when all of them fail: a healthy machine no
      longer polls Telegram just to find out that it has internet, and six
      providers decide the answer instead of one
    - Rotate optional pinned api.telegram.org addresses through --resolve
      while keeping DNS authoritative, and let the send fall back to them only
      when the resolver itself failed (curl rc=6)
    - Exit 75 (EX_TEMPFAIL) when the internet does not appear within
      TELEGRAM_MAX_WAIT, so the unit retries on that but not on permanent
      errors
    - Add telegram-send to add_dracutmodules so the module is included
  * debian/config/sshd.conf: drop readlink, basename, date and ifconfig from
    install_items - readlink and sleep already come from dracut's 80base
    module, ip(8) from 45net-lib, and nothing in the image uses the rest
  * Add debian/readme.dracut-modules.txt documenting the netplan,
    network-manager-wireless, telegram-send and unlock-root modules

 -- Eugene San (eugenesan) <eugenesan@gmail.com>  Tue, 06 Oct 2026 14:17:17 -0700

